counterfactuals.output:
The invention is a **variable-compression-ratio (VCR) connecting rod** whose effective length is hydraulically adjustable while the engine is operating.

The connecting rod has two relatively movable portions—such as a rod body and an extendable small-end member—or an eccentric bearing arrangement. Pressurized engine oil is routed into one or more hydraulic chambers within the rod. Electrically operated valves selectively admit, discharge, or transfer the oil, causing the mechanism to lengthen or shorten and then hydraulically lock in the selected position. A longer rod raises the piston’s top-dead-center position and increases compression ratio; a shorter rod lowers it and reduces compression ratio.

Because wiring a rapidly moving connecting rod is impractical, electrical power is supplied without physical contacts. A **stationary magnet mounted on the engine structure** produces a magnetic field through which an **induction device, such as a coil, carried by the connecting rod** repeatedly passes. Relative motion between the rod and magnetic field induces electrical energy in the coil. This energy may be rectified and stored locally in a capacitor, then used to operate the rod-mounted hydraulic valve or actuator. Control commands can likewise be provided magnetically or through timed energization of the stationary magnetic source.

The arrangement therefore combines:

- oil-pressure-driven connecting-rod length adjustment;
- hydraulic locking under combustion loads;
- rod-mounted electrically controlled valves;
- contactless generation or transfer of actuator power; and
- automatic selection of high compression for efficiency at light load and low compression to suppress knock at high load.

It avoids flexible wires, brushes, and slip contacts while allowing active compression-ratio control inside a conventional reciprocating engine.
